[17592186482495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186482341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482495 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482495 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482495 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482495 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e78defba-5c7e-59d1-ab03-e4daad3cbc4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482505 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482505 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482505 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $90$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "049efa61-2f95-573c-8d4c-3a15cf157276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482505 #edb/ref 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $90$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.9.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.9}) & ={\\int }_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{{x}_{0.9}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}} & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}}} & =0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}_{0.9}} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{0.9} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482508 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{195}+\\frac{38}{65}, & 2<x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07427b74-a9a0-5dbd-ab84-a95a81a836e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482508 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $F(2)=\\frac{2}{3}>\\frac{1}{2},$ we have $0\\le m\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482516 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26759ee4-9182-5088-bdc1-26b84fc02211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482516 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64},\\,0\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[3]{32}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482517 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482517 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482517 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482517 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $Y$ if the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{-6y},\\, & y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b515338-9a49-5088-8d83-3ec16988abce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482517 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $Y$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le m) & ={\\int }_{0}^{m}6{e}^{-6y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6y}{|}_{0}^{m} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {e}^{-6m} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -6m & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -6m & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482520 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482520 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482520 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482520 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $25$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-1,\\, & 2<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482520 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f4c7c35-e12b-53f3-8c46-ab140e4e9992" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482520 #edb/ref 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $25$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.25.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.25}) & ={\\int }_{2}^{{x}_{0.25}}(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x)|}_{2}^{{x}_{0.25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $25$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.25}^{2}}{4}-{x}_{0.25}+1 & =0.25 \\\\ {x}_{0.25}^{2}-4{x}_{0.25}+3 & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{0.25}-3)({x}_{0.25}-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{0.25}=1,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${x}_{0.25}=3$ because $2<{x}_{0.25}<4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482525 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $51.2$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36945eec-4c74-5f74-8f42-dd9a1f9a922a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482525 #edb/ref 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $51.2$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.512.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.512}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{{x}_{0.512}}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{x}^{\\frac{3}{2}}|}_{0}^{{x}_{0.512}} \\\\  & ={x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{3}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $51.2$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{3}{2}} & =0.512 \\\\ {x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{0.512} \\\\ {x}_{0.512}^{\\frac{1}{2}} & =0.8 \\\\ {x}_{0.512} & =(0.8)^{2} \\\\ {x}_{0.512} & =0.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482532 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<4, \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ca6810e-a219-54cd-a8c8-f575cfccdc55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482532 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{q}_{1}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{16}{{q}_{1}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{q}_{3}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{16}{{q}_{3}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=8-\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482543 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $21$st percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{21}x,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "537b608f-d718-5a66-8b0b-7dabfac0375b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482543 #edb/ref 1074 "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${x}_{p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {x}_{p})=p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $21$st percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.21.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.21}) & ={\\int }_{2}^{{x}_{0.21}}\\frac{2}{21}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{21}|}_{2}^{{x}_{0.21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.21}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{4}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.21}^{2}}{21}-\\frac{4}{21} & =0.21 \\\\ {x}_{0.21}^{2} & =(0.21)(21)+4 \\\\ {x}_{0.21} & =2.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482548 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "714df0ea-5d53-53df-b0aa-14ba037b9bde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482548 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{-1/2}{|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{m} & =2 \\\\ m & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482551 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482551 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{240}({x}^{4}-16), & 2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72db930f-dc67-5772-9a58-607de1ef27a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482551 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16),\\,2\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[4]{136}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $2\\le m\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482556 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e41b2e9-6ace-571f-a3ef-6f43b41df0ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482556 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ because $0\\le {q}_{1}\\le 1.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ because $0\\le {q}_{3}\\le 1.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.366\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482562 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{98}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 7, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d19c0b1-13a1-5100-8957-4c982f273723" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482562 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{7}^{m}\\frac{98}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{49}{{x}^{2}}|}_{7}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{{m}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{49}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{49}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m>7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482570 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}, & 0\\le x\\le 12, \\\\ 1, & x>12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a12ce635-7077-5ae6-9899-fd940aea64f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482570 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{144},\\,0\\le m\\le 12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{144}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=6\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482571 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482571 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482571 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482571 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8}, & 1\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4e71952-c18f-5e5a-8bbb-dfd0f7ad678f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482571 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{1}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2}-1 & =2 \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ because $1\\le {q}_{1}\\le 3.$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{q}_{3}^{2}-1}{8} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2}-1 & =6 \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =7 \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we take the root ${q}_{3}=\\sqrt{7}$ because $1\\le {q}_{3}\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & ≈0.914\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482575 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482575 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482575 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482575 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{6}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482575 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6d47759-e842-511b-a810-c9880823e33f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482575 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{m}^{6}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =\\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482591 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482591 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482591 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482591 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482591 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc24bfb9-272f-54fa-b546-5cdc700f6030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482591 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25},\\,0\\le m\\le 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482602 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfe1ae13-0566-5996-84a0-b50d07468365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482602 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{{q}_{1}}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{{q}_{1}}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{{q}_{3}}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{{q}_{3}}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR={q}_{3}-{q}_{1}=2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}≈0.845\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94639" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482613 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}},\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4cab15e-7525-590b-aa74-c49be38d3cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482613 #edb/ref 1074 "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{m}\\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}{|}_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{m} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8{m}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {m}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
